{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"elimination theorem elimination theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md","fileName":"Elimination Theorem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Elimination Theorem","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 19:45\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Polynomial Algorithms and Elimination]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Polynomial Algorithms and Elimination","Elimination Ideal","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"elimination theorem elimination theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md","fileName":"Elimination Theorem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Elimination Theorem","heading":"Elimination Theorem","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The elimination theorem states that if G is a Gröbner basis in lexicographic order x₁ > ··· > xₙ, then the elements of G involving only xₗ₊₁,…,xₙ form a Gröbner basis for the ℓ-th [[Elimination Ideal]].\n\nThe theorem turns variable removal into basis selection after one computation. Its geometric counterpart describes the Zariski closure of a projected affine variety.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Polynomial Algorithms and Elimination","Elimination Ideal","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"elimination theorem elimination theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Elimination Theorem.md","fileName":"E